# chapel — the private oval chapel in the south-west corner of the main block: an oval on the
#          cross axis, gilt and stucco over white, a blind ribbed cupola, and stained glass.
#
# ================================================================================================
# WHAT THIS PART PUTS UNDER LOAD — do not "tidy" any of it away.
#
# 1. A COLOURED VOLUME OF GLASS THROWING ITS COLOUR ON A FLOOR.
#
#    Four stained lights, 0.09 m thick, of `glass_ruby`, `glass_gold` and `glass_blue`. Those three
#    are ior 1.52 with Beer-Lambert `absorb=` per metre, and _contract.clip's own note says a
#    stained window made of `rgb` alone is a coloured SURFACE and one made of `absorb` is a coloured
#    VOLUME. This is the only place in the building either is tested. 0.09 m is deliberately between
#    the contract's two poles — 12 mm is a tint, 300 mm is a colour — so the answer here is a
#    QUANTITY and not a yes/no: a pane this thick should tint, not stain.
#
#    It is a two-bounce question and both halves are set up for on purpose. The direct half is the
#    patch on the floor: the pavement under each light is plain `travertine` (214,196,164), warm and
#    pale and unpatterned, so a red patch on it is a red patch and not an argument with a red
#    pavement. The indirect half is the WHITE stucco above the entablature, which has nothing on it
#    at all: if the second bounce carries spectrum, the vault over the south-west light goes pink
#    and the vault over the east light goes cold, four metres apart in one frame. If the vault comes
#    back one neutral white the bounce is carrying luminance only.
#
# 2. A BLIND LANTERN — light that arrives only after two bounces.
#
#    The piano nobile floor is at 6.30 and this room's ceiling may not pass 6.00, so the cupola
#    cannot break through and the lantern has NO OPENING TO THE SKY. It is a closed chamber 1.08 m
#    across and 0.30 m tall, reached only through an eye 0.72 m across in the crown of the cupola,
#    and the only light that will ever be in it is light that came through a stained window, bounced
#    off the floor, went up through the eye and bounced again. That is a third bounce, not a second.
#    The whole upper third of this room — everything above 5.175 — is second-bounce light, and the
#    lantern chamber is third. It converges or it does not; there is no other way in.
#
#    DO NOT PUT A LIGHT IN THE LANTERN and do not open it. No flame in this room is above 3.30 for
#    the same reason: a taper level with the entablature would light the cupola directly and the
#    whole of this would stop being a second-bounce problem.
#
# 3. TRANSLUCENT STONE. Four giant pilasters and the apse lining are `alabaster`, translucent=210 —
#    stone you can see INTO, and the other half of the pair the contract names, `marble` at 110
#    being the first half. Each pilaster carries an ormolu bracket on its face at 2.25 with a taper
#    on it, its flame 0.0675 clear of 0.135 m of translucent stone at a height of 2.60: a small hot
#    source hard against a thin slab of alabaster. If the subsurface term is doing anything at all
#    the pilaster glows around the flame. A pilaster is the right host and a wall is not — it is thin
#    enough to transmit and thick enough to scatter, and there are four of them to compare.
#
# 4. THIN MATTER AT ONE AND TWO VOXELS, at metre 32 where a voxel is 0.03125 m:
#      glazing bars      0.045 wide, 0.0225 proud   1.4 voxels
#      cupola ribs       0.06 wide, 0.09 proud      1.9 voxels
#      rail balusters    0.06 across at the neck    1.9 voxels
#      tapers            0.09 across                2.9 voxels, and 1.4 at metre 16
#      gilt fillet       0.045 tall                 1.4 voxels
#      architrave step   0.0225 in z                SUB-VOXEL, and meant to be
#    None of them is decoration that happens to be small. They are the sampler's question.
#
#    THE TAPERS WERE 0.045 AND ARE 0.09, AND THAT IS A CONCESSION THAT WAS MEASURED. At 0.045 the
#    wax sheds at metre 16 while its flame does not, and this file reported 48 floating voxels of
#    emissive hanging in mid-air over the altar. The socket under each taper is the general fix —
#    0.18 of ormolu buried 0.09 into whatever the taper stands on — and the wax was doubled on top
#    of it so the column either stands on metal that is still there or is gone entirely.
#
# MEASURED, with `clips/facility/requests/chapel-probe.clip`, which restates `bounds` as this zone
# plus a third of a metre (see that file — the contract's box will not fit at full detail):
#
#   metre 32   2,719,036 voxels   82.98 m3   352.46 m2 of surface   22 materials   ONE component
#   metre 16     327,360 voxels                    THREE components, 56 floating -- read on
#   metre  8      41,945 voxels                                                    ONE component
#
# It was 3,163,047 voxels and 318.87 m2 over 19 materials. The volume went down and the surface up:
# the room is 0.45 m deeper, the four embrasures start 0.80 further out — both of those are block
# this file no longer occupies — and the stalls, the paving joints, the turned balusters, the
# altar's sunk arcade and the wax are all surface rather than mass. `--part part_chapel --metre 8`
# on the manifest reports ONE COMPONENT, 41,945 voxels and no rule that never fired.
#
# THE 56 VOXELS AT METRE 16 ARE THE TWO RAIL BALUSTERS AND I COULD NOT MAKE THEM GO. The neck is
# 0.06 and the belly 0.12; at metre 16 a voxel is 0.0625 and the four voxels round the axis stand
# 0.0425 out from it, so the belly is sampled and the neck and the collar above it are not, and what
# is left is the belly with nothing holding it — 28 voxels, twice. It is clean at metre 32 and clean
# at metre 8, which is where a voxel is coarse enough that nothing of the baluster survives at all.
# Thickening the neck to 0.09 would fix metre 16 and cost this file the thin-matter case its own
# header lists; narrowing the belly to under 0.085 would fix it with a 0.5 mm margin, which is not a
# fix. So it stands, measured and written down, at the one metre between the contract's and the
# coarse one.
#
# ONE COMPONENT AT EVERY METRE, AND THAT TOOK FOUR SEPARATE FIXES, EVERY ONE OF THEM A JOINT THAT
# TOUCHED WITHOUT LAPPING. Written down because each looked like a different bug and all four were
# the brief's rule 4:
#
#   the altar mensa      bedded on the altar at exactly 2.610 — 60 voxels of mensa, tapers and all,
#                        adrift at metre 16. Now 2.565, lapping 0.045.
#   the tapers           0.045 of wax sheds at metre 16 and its flame does not. Fixed with the
#                        ormolu socket and by doubling the wax; the flame was also brought down so
#                        it laps the wick by 0.087 instead of 0.042, which is more than a voxel.
#   the cupola ribs      a lowered copy of the intrados makes a shell that runs to nothing at the
#                        springing. `offset` along the normal instead.
#   the candelabra       0.045 from the rail, they bridged to it at metre 8 through a blob that
#                        touched neither the floor nor the rail's own footing. Moved to stand
#                        against the altar, which they should have done anyway.
#
# WHAT THE PROBE SAYS ABOUT WALKING IN HERE, at metre 32, before this pass and after. Every one is
# `clipcheck --gap`'s `clear` line — the longest unbroken run of AIR with stone at both ends — and
# not `--span`, which reports matter and answers a different question:
#
# The three columns are: as this room stood before any of this; with the stalls and the gate in it
# at 2.25 deep; and now, at 2.70 deep with the cutters fixed.
#
#   the entrance passage, across   --gap z@-7.00,2.50      1.375   1.375   1.375
#   the entrance passage, up       --gap y@-7.00,-4.50     2.688   2.688   2.688
#   the rail at floor level        --gap z@-13.05,1.85     0.344   1.562   1.562
#   the gate                       --gap z@-12.375,1.85      —     1.125   1.125
#   the gate at rail height        --gap z@-12.375,2.20      —     1.125   1.125
#   the nave past the stalls       --gap z@-11.00,2.10     2.062   1.688   2.062
#   the nave at a stall arm        --gap z@-10.80,2.40     2.250   1.438   1.750
#   the nave at the bay end        --gap z@-11.90,2.10     1.938   1.500   1.750
#   the nave between the pilasters --gap z@-12.15,2.40     1.625   1.625   2.062
#   the floor across the nave      --gap z@-10.80,1.85       —       —     1.750
#   altar to entrance, along       --gap x@2.20,-4.50      7.281   7.750   7.750
#   head over the stalls           --gap y@-10.80,-5.30    3.656   3.000   3.719
#   head in the nave               --gap y@-10.80,-4.10    3.781   3.781   3.781
#   head in the apse               --gap y@-14.30,-4.50    2.469   2.469   2.469
#
# The middle column is what four stalls cost a room 2.25 m deep: 1.438 at the tightest, which is
# over the 1.20 a path owes and not by much. The right-hand column is the same four stalls in a room
# 2.70 deep. Nothing in this room is now under 1.375 anywhere a person goes.
#
# THE 0.344 IS THE ONE THAT MATTERED AND IT IS NOT A NARROW GAP, IT IS NO GAP AT ALL: it is the air
# OUTSIDE the building, reported OPEN at the near end, because at x = -13.05 the old rail ran from
# wall to wall and there was no air inside this room on that line. The gate is 1.125 now. The stalls
# take the nave from 2.062 to 1.438 at their tightest, which is what furniture in a room 2.25 deep
# costs and is still 0.238 over the 1.20 a path owes.
#
# AND THE FOUR LIGHTS. `clips/facility.clip` ITSELF CANNOT ANSWER THIS AND IT IS WORTH SAYING WHY,
# because two hours went into finding it out: `--gap` reports the LONGEST run of air on its line, and
# on the real building the longest run through a window is the sky. Along z at the level of the glass
# it is the 9 m of outside air; along y it is everything above the cornice; along x it is another
# room's floor — `--gap x@-0.20,-6.45` on the building reports 7.750 m, and that is somebody else's
# room, not this one's embrasure. A question about one opening has to be asked in a box that holds
# one opening. So: a scratch probe carrying walls.clip, windows.clip and this file with
# `void_windows` applied, cut to the window, with the cutters the building actually has:
#
#   south light   --gap y@-13.50,-6.20   2.062  closed   the column of air inside my own embrasure,
#                                                        0.30 north of where a 1.00 cutter stops
#   south light   --gap z@-13.50,3.30    1.125  open     the depth of the opening, from windows.clip's
#                                                        own sash to within 0.03 of my pane
#   west light    --gap z@-14.95,3.30    1.812  closed   the splay, 0.05 east of the cutter's end
#
# and the same three, with the embrasures as they were before this pass — built to meet a 1.80
# cutter that no longer reaches — read 0.688 and 0.625: 0.80 m of my own block standing between the
# opening and the pane, which is the room going dark. That is why the embrasures are cut to the
# wall's line and not to any cutter's.
#
# `void_chapel` reports nine components and that is CORRECT and must not be "fixed". Eight of them
# are the 0.03 m air gaps between each stained light and the window cutter behind it — the cavity
# between an outer sash and an inner light — plus two one-voxel pockets behind the retable. A void
# is air; air in a room is allowed to be in more than one pocket. It is `part_chapel` that has to be
# one piece, and it is.
#
# ================================================================================================
# THE MODULE. D = 0.90, M = 0.45, and every number below is a multiple of M or of M/2, M/4, M/5,
# M/10 or M/20. Written out so nobody has to work backwards:
#
#   -10.80  oval centre in x       24 M       1.665  paving foot, bedded 0.135 into the podium
#    -4.50  oval centre in z       10 M       1.800  FLOOR                    podium_top
#     3.60  oval semi-axis in x     8 M       2.250  dado top                 1.80 + M
#     1.350 oval semi-axis in z       3 M     2.700  window sills             windows.clip's
#     0.675 apse and door radius  1.5 M       4.050  window springing         1.80 + 5 M
#                                             4.500  square window heads      windows.clip's
#   The room in the clear is 7.20 x 2.70 —    4.725  ARCHITRAVE BED           1.80 + 6.5 M
#   16 M by 6 M — with the long axis east-    5.175  cornice top, springing   4.725 + M
#   west, the altar in the west apse and      5.625  cupola crown             5.175 + M
#   the door in the east.                     5.880  lantern crown            5.625 + 0.255
#                                             6.000  the limit. 0.12 of stone over the lantern.
#
#   and the four stalls, which are new:       2.205  under the stall seat     1.80 + 0.9 M
#     k = 0.72 the panelled front             2.295  the seat                 1.80 + 1.1 M
#     k = 0.68 the seat and its nosing        2.475  the arms that divide it  1.80 + 1.5 M
#     k = 0.64 the arms                       2.700  the stall back dies on windows.clip's sill
#     k = 0.94 the panelled back
#
# WHY THE ARCHITRAVE BED IS AT 4.725 AND NOT AT 4.05, WHICH IS WHERE IT WANTS TO BE.
#
# Because windows.clip's arched ground-storey window at x = -8.10 has its head at 4.725, and its
# cutter is a cylinder that takes out everything of mine below that line across 1.35 m. An
# entablature bedded at 4.05 comes back with an arched notch a metre wide bitten out of it, and the
# notch is not mine to refuse: `void_windows` is subtracted from the whole building after this file
# is assembled. So the order is stretched to 6.5 M and the cupola is squeezed to 0.45 of rise. That
# is not the section anybody would draw first; it is the section this corner of this building has
# room for, and the flat cupola is the honest consequence rather than a mistake.
#
# WHY THE ROOM WAS 2.25 DEEP AND IS 2.70 NOW. THIS IS THE ENTRY WORTH READING.
#
# Every ground-storey window in this building used to be punched 1.80 m in from the OUTER wall face
# — 0.90 m PAST the inner face of a 0.90 m wall — and that hole is subtracted from the whole
# building after this file is assembled. So the strip z -6.65 .. -5.70 along my whole south side was
# a hole I did not control, anything I built in it was deleted, and the oval's south flank stood at
# z = -5.625, three quarters of a voxel clear of that line. halls.clip lost 1.15 m down each end of
# the building to the same number. It was never a wall thickness anybody had measured; it was a
# guess at what walls.clip might turn out to be.
#
# walls.clip has now written it down — `wall_thick` 0.90, `wall_cut` 1.00, `wall_relief` 0.18, in
# requests/walls-thickness.md — and windows.clip and doors.clip cut 1.00. Nothing reaches past
# z = -6.50 any more. So:
#
#   the oval's z semi-axis   1.125 (2.5 M)  ->  1.350 (3 M)      the room 2.25 deep -> 2.70
#   the south flank          -5.625         ->  -5.850
#   the north flank          -3.375         ->  -3.150           0.20 of wall to my block's face
#   the three south lights   -5.67 .. -5.58 ->  -6.03 .. -5.94
#
# THE LIGHTS HAD TO MOVE AND THAT IS NOT COSMETIC. The middle one stood at -5.67, which the oval now
# reaches past: at x = -10.80 the flank is at -5.850, so the pane was left standing in mid-air INSIDE
# the room. Measured, that is exactly what the report said — 1,396 voxels adrift at metre 16 whose
# low corner was x = -11.50, the pane's own west edge. They sit at -6.03 .. -5.94 now: 0.09 south of
# the oval's southernmost point, so they are inside the wall at every x across the opening, and 0.47
# north of where a 1.00 cutter stops, so nothing outside this file can reach them.
#
# The embrasures did not have to change at all, because they were already cut to the wall's own line
# rather than to any cutter's — see the embrasure section, and the table further down measuring the
# four lights under both cutters.
#
# WHERE IT MEETS ITS NEIGHBOURS:
#
#   south   the outer wall, inner face z = -6.60. My block runs to -6.65, lapping it by 0.05.
#   west    the outer wall, inner face x = -15.10. My block runs to -15.15.
#   below   the podium, top 1.80. The paving is bedded 0.135 into it.
#   north   halls.clip's west hall BLOCK, whose south face is at z = -2.925 (its own header: the
#           block faces are at +-2.925, the room faces at +-2.025). My zone stops at -3.00 and
#           allows 0.05 of lap, so my north face is at -2.95 and there is a 0.025 m cavity between
#           the two. It is closed on both sides by stone, opens into no room, and is under a voxel
#           at the contract's metre; closing it would mean leaving the zone. Deliberate.
#   east    the lesser south door, whose void is x -6.30 .. -4.50, z -7.95 .. -5.70, head 4.95. It
#           lands in the unbuilt lobby between the vestibule's west wall (x = -4.50) and my east
#           face (x = -6.00). My entrance passage opens on that lobby at x = -6.00, z -5.175 ..
#           -3.825, with 2.70 m of clear head — so a person comes north through the lesser door,
#           turns west, and is in the chapel. Nothing of mine crosses x = -6.00.
#
# HOW THE OVAL IS MADE, since the language has no `from=`/`to=` on `revolve` yet. Every oval in this
# file is `scale { cylinder r=1 } x=3.60*k z=1.125*k` — exact, and concentric ovals are one scalar
# apart. That is why the mouldings project 3.2 times as far at the ends of the room as on its
# flanks: a scaled oval offset by scaling is not offset by a constant, and the alternative,
# `offset`, gives the same answer because its metric is scaled too (field.cpp scales a non-uniform
# scale's distance by the SMALLEST factor). It is what an oval room built this way looks like and it
# reads as continuous, which is what the eye is measuring.
#
# EVERY NON-UNIFORM `scale` AND EVERY `rotate` IN THIS FILE IS WRAPPED IN AN INTERSECTION WITH A BOX
# IT ALREADY FITS INSIDE, AND THAT IS LOAD-BEARING. `build_bounds` answers `everywhere()` for a
# non-uniform scale (field.cpp: `if (most - least > 1e-12) { box = everywhere(); break; }`) exactly
# as it does for a rotate, the sampler takes the minimum slack over the whole expression, and
# infinite slack anywhere means not one voxel of the clip's 582-million-cell array can be skipped
# ANYWHERE. windows.clip measured that substitution at 705 million field evaluations against 5.4
# seconds. The boxes below are bounds, not cutters; nothing touches their faces.

include "_contract.clip"

# ================================================================================================
# THE OVALS — one family, one scalar apart
# ================================================================================================
#
# k = 1.00 is the room. Each step of 0.04 takes 0.045 off the semi-axis in z and 0.144 in x.

let chapel_ov_100 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.600 z=1.3500 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.45 0.25 -5.905   -7.15 7.55 -3.095
}
let chapel_ov_096 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.456 z=1.2960 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.30 0.25 -5.865   -7.30 7.55 -3.135
}
let chapel_ov_094 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.384 z=1.2690 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.23 0.25 -5.845   -7.37 7.55 -3.155
}
let chapel_ov_092 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.312 z=1.2420 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.15 0.25 -5.825   -7.45 7.55 -3.175
}
let chapel_ov_088 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.168 z=1.1880 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.00 0.25 -5.775   -7.60 7.55 -3.225
}
let chapel_ov_030 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=1.080 z=0.4050 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -11.92 0.25 -4.94   -9.68 7.55 -4.06
}

# THE FOUR THE STALLS ARE MADE OF, on the same family and the same rule. k = 0.76 is the ground a
# panel is sunk to, 0.72 the stall front, 0.68 the seat's nosing, 0.64 the arms that divide it.
# Every one of them is boxed for the reason in the header: a non-uniform scale reports infinite
# bounds and costs the whole clip its skip.
let chapel_ov_076 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=2.736 z=1.0260 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -13.60 0.25 -5.625   -8.00 7.55 -3.375
}
let chapel_ov_072 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=2.592 z=0.9720 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -13.45 0.25 -5.585   -8.15 7.55 -3.415
}
let chapel_ov_068 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=2.448 z=0.9180 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -13.30 0.25 -5.535   -8.30 7.55 -3.465
}
let chapel_ov_064 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=2.304 z=0.8640 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -13.15 0.25 -5.485   -8.45 7.55 -3.515
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE AIR OF THE ROOM
# ================================================================================================
#
# The nave to the cornice, the cupola over it, the eye, the blind lantern chamber, the west apse,
# the east entrance passage, and the four window embrasures. It is one connected volume; every
# piece below overlaps its neighbour.

let chapel_nave_air = intersection { chapel_ov_100  box -14.45 1.80 -5.905   -7.15 5.175 -3.095 }

# THE CUPOLA. Semi-axes 3.168 x 0.99 in plan — the cornice's own inner face, k = 0.88 — and 0.45 of
# rise. Springing 5.175, crown 5.625.
let chapel_dome_air = intersection {
    translate { scale { ellipsoid 0 0 0 rx=3.168 ry=0.450 rz=1.188 } x=1 z=1 } -10.80 5.175 -4.50
    box -14.00 5.170 -5.75   -7.60 5.63 -3.25
}

# THE EYE, 0.72 across (1.6 M), and the BLIND chamber over it. The bore runs from 5.40 to 5.775 and
# the intrados crowns at 5.625, so 0.15 of it stands clear of the dome as a neck; the chamber over
# that is 1.08 across and 0.18 tall, and its crown is at 5.88 with 0.12 of stone above it and no way
# out. Everything in it arrives through the eye, and everything that arrives at the eye has already
# bounced twice.
let chapel_eye_air  = cylinder -10.80 5.5875 -4.50  r=0.360 h=0.375 axis=y
let chapel_lant_air = cylinder -10.80 5.7450 -4.50  r=0.540 h=0.090 axis=y
let chapel_lant_cap = ellipsoid -10.80 5.7900 -4.50  rx=0.540 ry=0.090 rz=0.540

# THE WEST APSE. A cylinder of 1.5 M struck on x = -14.175, so it stands 0.45 west of the oval's own
# vertex at -14.40 and leaves 0.30 of stone behind it before my block face at -15.15. Springing
# 3.60, and a half-dome flattened to 0.45 of rise so its crown lands on the architrave bed.
#
# HEAD HEIGHT IN THE APSE IS 2.185 AT 0.35 FROM ITS AXIS AND 2.006 AT THE BACK WALL, WHICH IS UNDER
# THE BRIEF'S 2.10 AND IS MEANT TO BE. The retable stands in the back 0.45 of it: there is nowhere
# to stand under the low part, by construction. In front of the retable, and everywhere else in this
# room, the clear height is 2.925 at the wall and 3.825 in the middle.
let chapel_apse_air = union {
    cylinder  -14.175 2.700 -4.50  r=0.675 h=1.80 axis=y
    ellipsoid -14.175 3.600 -4.50  rx=0.675 ry=0.450 rz=0.675
}

# THE EAST ENTRANCE PASSAGE. 1.35 wide (3 M) on the room's own axis, springing 3.825, semicircular
# head crowning at 4.50 — 2.70 m of clear head, against the brief's 2.40 through a door. It starts
# at x = -7.65, which is 0.27 west of where the oval's east flank stands on the jamb lines, so the
# two lap and the doorway is cut out of the oval wall rather than butted against it.
let chapel_pass_air = union {
    box      -7.65 1.80 -5.175   -6.00 3.825 -3.825
    cylinder -6.825 3.825 -4.50  r=0.675 h=1.65 axis=x
}

# THE FOUR EMBRASURES, AND THEY ARE BUILT OFF THE WALL AND NOT OFF THE CUTTER NOW.
#
# They used to start at z = -5.70 and x = -14.20, which are not features of this building: they are
# where windows.clip's cutter happened to stop, quoted here as two bare numbers. requests/
# walls-thickness.md settles what the wall actually is — 0.90 thick, inner face on z = -6.60 and
# x = -15.10, and a cutter needs `wall_cut` = 1.00 from the outer face to come through it with the
# 0.10 of slack that file uses everywhere. So the embrasures start at z = -6.50 and x = -15.00: the
# line an opening ENDS on, derived from the wall, and 0.10 inside the face rather than 0.90 past it.
#
# THAT MAKES THIS FRAGMENT RIGHT UNDER BOTH CUTTERS AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT OF DOING IT THIS WAY.
# windows.clip and doors.clip still punch 1.80 as I write this — `windows_pn_cut` and
# `windows_gs_cut` both read `... 1.80` on origin/main, and requests/walls-thickness.md is a request
# to those files, not a record of a change to them. With the 1.80 cutter live my embrasure is
# swallowed by it and nothing changes; when the cutter becomes 1.00 the opening ends at -6.50 and my
# embrasure begins there. Either way the four lights are lit. Built the other way round — meeting the
# 1.00 line only — this room would have gone dark the moment the request was honoured, and it is that
# fault the coordinator was told had already happened.
#
# 1.35 wide (3 M) — the openings' own width — sill on windows.clip's 2.70, springing 4.05 (1.80+5M),
# semicircular head of 1.5 M crowning at 4.725, which is the architrave bed. The one arched window in
# this zone, at x = -8.10, has exactly that head, so the embrasure and the opening agree.
let chapel_emb_s_a = union {
    box      -14.175 2.70 -6.50   -12.825 4.05 -4.50
    cylinder -13.500 4.05 -5.50   r=0.675 h=2.00 axis=z
}
let chapel_emb_s_b = union {
    box      -11.475 2.70 -6.50   -10.125 4.05 -4.50
    cylinder -10.800 4.05 -5.50   r=0.675 h=2.00 axis=z
}
let chapel_emb_s_c = union {
    box       -8.775 2.70 -6.50    -7.425 4.05 -4.50
    cylinder  -8.100 4.05 -5.50   r=0.675 h=2.00 axis=z
}
# The west light stands in a wall that is 1.20 thick at its north jamb and 2.19 at its south, so its
# embrasure is a splay and not a tunnel: it opens north-east into the room, and its southern quarter
# is a blind pocket because the oval has already turned away behind it.
let chapel_emb_w = union {
    box      -15.000 2.70 -6.075  -12.900 4.05 -4.275
    cylinder -13.950 4.05 -5.400  r=0.675 h=2.100 axis=x
}

# THE JOINTS IN THE PAVING, WHICH ARE AIR AND NOT PAINT.
#
# This floor was one continuous solid with three colours drawn on it, and a floor is not that: it is
# slabs, and what tells the eye so is the shadow in the joint between two of them. So the joints are
# cut — 0.03 wide and 0.03 deep, one voxel each way at the contract's metre and gone by metre 16,
# which is exactly the right way for a joint to behave — on a grid of 0.45 (M) struck on the room's
# own centre. They belong to the air because that is what a groove is, and because a void written as
# the air LESS the part then carries them without another line.
#
# THE GRID IS CAUGHT IN k = 0.88 AND THAT IS NOT TIDINESS, IT IS THE TWO THINGS A GROOVE CAN BREAK.
# The `breccia` border already runs on that line and a border is one stone, so stopping the joints
# there is what a paved floor does anyway — but the reason it is written that way is measured. Cut
# to a plain box instead:
#
#   a box edge 0.05 short of the block's east face put TWO VOXELS OF MATTER THROUGH that face, at
#   x -6.00 .. -5.94, 1.8 m apart up the wall. Pulling the edge in made them go.
#   a groove landing on x = -7.65 — the entrance passage's own west face, and a joint of the 0.45
#   grid struck on the room's centre — cut the foot from the 0.015 sliver of wall between the oval
#   and that face and left 104 VOXELS FLOATING in two islands at metre 32. A groove 0.03 deep
#   cannot break a floor 0.135 thick; it can break anything standing ON the floor whose own
#   footprint is thinner than the groove is wide, and the tail of an oval is full of those.
#
# k = 0.88 clears the oval's wall by 0.135 on the flanks and 0.432 at the ends, so no groove reaches
# the foot of anything.
let chapel_fl_jt_x = translate {
    repeat { box -0.015 1.770 -7.00   0.015 1.8005 -2.60 } x=0.45 nx=11
} -10.80 0 0
let chapel_fl_jt_z = translate {
    repeat { box -15.50 1.770 -0.015  -5.70 1.8005 0.015 } z=0.45 nz=5
} 0 0 -4.50
let chapel_fl_joints = intersection {
    union { chapel_fl_jt_x chapel_fl_jt_z }
    chapel_ov_088
}

let chapel_air = union {
    chapel_nave_air chapel_dome_air chapel_eye_air chapel_lant_air chapel_lant_cap
    chapel_apse_air chapel_pass_air
    chapel_emb_s_a chapel_emb_s_b chapel_emb_s_c chapel_emb_w
    chapel_fl_joints
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE MASS
# ================================================================================================
#
# The whole zone, less the air. Everything between the oval and the rectangle is solid: that is the
# 0.975 m of south wall the embrasures are cut through, the 0.75 m the apse is sunk into, and the
# floor of the storey above. It laps the podium by 0.135 below, the outer walls by 0.05 west and
# south, and stops at -2.95 on the north for the reason in the header.
let chapel_block = box -15.15 1.665 -6.65   -6.00 6.00 -2.95  round=0.02
let chapel_mass  = difference { chapel_block chapel_air }

# ================================================================================================
# THE ORDER
# ================================================================================================
#
# THE DADO, 1.80 to 2.25 (M), face on k = 0.92 — 0.09 proud on the flanks, 0.288 at the ends — with
# a fillet cap on k = 0.88. It is the one horizontal that runs unbroken round the whole room under
# the windows, and it is what the rail and the candelabra stand against.
let chapel_dado_body = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_092 }
    box -14.45 1.80 -5.905   -7.15 2.160 -3.095
}
let chapel_dado_cap = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_088 }
    box -14.45 2.160 -5.905   -7.15 2.250 -3.095
}
# THE DADO STOPS AT x = -7.875 AND THAT IS NOT AN ARBITRARY TRIM. East of it the oval closes on its
# own vertex at -7.20 and the entrance arch takes the whole width, so the band between k = 1.00 and
# k = 0.92 comes out as a pair of crumbs a few voxels across with nothing to hold them — 11 floating
# voxels at metre 24, measured. The dado dies against the door architrave instead, which is what a
# dado does at a doorway anyway.
let chapel_dado = intersection {
    union { chapel_dado_body chapel_dado_cap }
    box -14.45 1.79 -5.905   -8.100 2.26 -3.095
}

# THE FOUR GIANT PILASTERS, of `alabaster`, at x = -12.15 and -9.45 on both flanks — the two lines
# that divide this room into three bays, apse, centre and entrance, and the only two places on the
# south flank where there is wall between two windows to stand one on. 0.45 wide (M), standing 0.135
# proud of the oval, 1.80 to 4.725. The back face of each is buried in the wall, so no pilaster can
# come adrift whatever the oval does behind it.
#
# The oval's flank at x = -12.15 and -9.45 stands at z = -5.543 and -3.457; the box faces at -5.408
# and -3.592 are therefore 0.135 proud at the middle of each pilaster and 0.104 at its arrises,
# which is the small entasis a flat pilaster on a curved wall has anyway.
let chapel_pil_s_a = box -12.375 1.80 -5.8085  -11.925 4.500 -5.6165 round=0.010
let chapel_pil_s_b = box  -9.675 1.80 -5.8085   -9.225 4.500 -5.6165 round=0.010
let chapel_pil_n_a = box -12.375 1.80 -3.3835  -11.925 4.500 -3.1915 round=0.010
let chapel_pil_n_b = box  -9.675 1.80 -3.3835   -9.225 4.500 -3.1915 round=0.010
let chapel_pil_shafts = union {
    chapel_pil_s_a chapel_pil_s_b chapel_pil_n_a chapel_pil_n_b
}

# The capitals: a plain abacus of M/2 with a fillet under it, 4.50 to 4.725, breaking forward 0.045
# past the shaft on every side. Ionic volutes at this width would be 0.09 across and would not
# survive the metre; a cushion capital is what a small oval chapel of this century has anyway.
let chapel_cap_s_a = box -12.420 4.500 -5.8535  -11.880 4.725 -5.6165 round=0.008
let chapel_cap_s_b = box  -9.720 4.500 -5.8535   -9.180 4.725 -5.6165 round=0.008
let chapel_cap_n_a = box -12.420 4.500 -3.3835  -11.880 4.725 -3.1465 round=0.008
let chapel_cap_n_b = box  -9.720 4.500 -3.3835   -9.180 4.725 -3.1465 round=0.008
let chapel_capitals = union {
    chapel_cap_s_a chapel_cap_s_b chapel_cap_n_a chapel_cap_n_b
}
let chapel_pilasters = union { chapel_pil_shafts chapel_capitals }

# THE ENTABLATURE, 4.725 to 5.175 — the contract's 2.00 m reduced to M, which is the whole of what
# this ceiling height allows. Three bands and not one: architrave on k = 0.96, frieze set back on
# k = 0.94, cornice breaking forward to k = 0.88. The step between architrave and frieze is 0.0225
# in z — sub-voxel at metre 32, exactly as the rotunda's coffer lips are, and for the same reason.
let chapel_arch_band = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_096 }
    box -14.45 4.725 -5.905   -7.15 4.905 -3.095
}
let chapel_frieze_band = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_094 }
    box -14.45 4.905 -5.905   -7.15 5.040 -3.095
}
let chapel_cornice_band = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_088 }
    box -14.45 5.040 -5.905   -7.15 5.175 -3.095
}
let chapel_entab = union { chapel_arch_band chapel_frieze_band chapel_cornice_band }

# THE GILT FILLET under the cornice: 0.045 tall, breaking 0.045 further forward than the frieze. One
# voxel and a half of metal running right round an oval, on the line the eye follows.
let chapel_fillet = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_092 }
    box -14.45 4.995 -5.905   -7.15 5.040 -3.095
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE STALLS — four of them, two to a flank, in the centre bay only
# ================================================================================================
#
# A chapel with an altar and no seat in it is a picture of a chapel. These are the seats, and they
# are also the one piece of carved joinery in this room: a panelled front with the panels really
# sunk 0.045, a seat with a nosing that really projects, and arms that really divide one stall from
# the next. Nothing about them is painted on.
#
# WHERE THEY ARE IS A WALKING NUMBER AND NOT A TASTE ONE. They stand between the two pilaster lines
# — x -11.925 to -9.675, which is the centre bay and the only stretch of this room with no window,
# no pilaster, no bracket and no taper on either flank. In front of the seat's nosing (k = 0.68)
# the room is 1.53 m across on the bay's own axis and 1.359 at its two ends; in front of the arms
# (k = 0.64) it is 1.44 and 1.257. All four are over the 1.20 m a person needs to pass furniture,
# and the pavement between them is the pavement the coloured light falls on, which is why the
# stalls stop at the pilasters and do not run under the windows.
#
#   1.755  the stalls are bedded 0.045 into the paving, so the joint is a lap and not a touch —
#          the four fixes in this file's header were every one of them a joint that touched
#   2.205  under the seat        k = 0.72   the panelled front, 0.315 proud of the wall
#   2.295  the seat              k = 0.68   0.045 of nosing over the panel below it
#   2.475  the arms              k = 0.64   0.045 again, and they stand 0.18 over the seat
#   2.700  the back              k = 0.94   dying on windows.clip's own sill line
let chapel_stall_zone = box -11.925 1.755 -5.905   -9.675 2.205 -3.095

# The front, and the four sunk panels in it. A panel is cut from the k = 0.72 face back to k = 0.76
# — 0.045 on the flanks, which is a voxel and a half at the contract's metre and the same depth the
# vault panels next door are sunk. Two panels to a flank, 0.90 (2 M) wide, between the three arms.
let chapel_stall_pan_box = union {
    box -11.8125 1.890 -6.00  -10.9125 2.115 -3.00
    box -10.6875 1.890 -6.00   -9.7875 2.115 -3.00
}
let chapel_stall_sink = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_076 chapel_ov_072 }
    chapel_stall_pan_box
}
let chapel_stall_front = difference {
    intersection { difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_072 } chapel_stall_zone }
    chapel_stall_sink
}

let chapel_stall_seat = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_068 }
    box -11.925 2.205 -5.905   -9.675 2.295 -3.095
}

# The arms. Three slabs 0.09 thick on the bay's own module — the two ends and the middle — each one
# reaching from the wall to 0.045 past the seat's nosing and standing 0.18 above it. `repeat` with
# nx=1 is three copies, which is what this wants; the slab is caught in the oval band so it follows
# the wall behind it instead of standing off it at the ends.
let chapel_stall_arm_x = translate {
    repeat { box -0.045 1.755 -6.00   0.045 2.475 -3.00 } x=1.125 nx=1
} -10.80 0 0
let chapel_stall_arms = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_064 }
    chapel_stall_arm_x
}

# The back: a panelled ground 0.0675 proud of the plaster, from the dado cap it laps into up to the
# window sills. It is behind the dado cap's own 0.135, so the cap reads as a shelf over it.
let chapel_stall_back = intersection {
    difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_094 }
    box -11.925 2.205 -5.905   -9.675 2.700 -3.095
}

let chapel_stalls = union {
    chapel_stall_front chapel_stall_seat chapel_stall_arms chapel_stall_back
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE CUPOLA'S EIGHT RIBS
# ================================================================================================
#
# Slabs 0.06 thick through the vertical axis, caught in the shell between the intrados and a copy of
# it 0.09 lower — so each rib stands 0.09 proud at the crown and dies into the cornice at the
# springing, which is what a rib does. Four slabs make eight ribs. They stop 0.06 short of the eye
# on every side, so the eye stays a clean ellipse of stucco and the ribs read as meeting a ring.
#
# The fan is written as four explicit rotations and NOT as `around { } count=8`: `metric_slack` for
# a polar repeat is infinite and would cost the whole clip its skip, which _order.clip measured at a
# factor of 950 on one column. Every rotation is boxed, for the reason in the header.
# THE SHELL THE RIBS ARE CUT OUT OF IS A TRUE NORMAL OFFSET AND NOT A LOWERED COPY. A copy of the
# intrados set down 0.09 gives a shell 0.09 thick at the crown and nothing at all at the springing,
# where the surface turns vertical — and what comes out of the thin end is single voxels with no
# neighbours (five of them at metre 24, measured, and eleven more before that). `offset` measures
# along the normal, so the shell is 0.09 thick everywhere and the ribs are 0.09 proud everywhere.
# `chapel_dome_air` is already caught in a box, so the offset is of a bounded shape and costs
# nothing extra — rotunda.clip measured that question and the answer was 749 ms against 751.
let chapel_dome_in = offset { chapel_dome_air } by=-0.090
let chapel_rib_shell = difference { chapel_dome_air chapel_dome_in }

let chapel_rib_bar = mirror { box 0.420 5.10 -0.030   3.300 5.70 0.030 } axis=x
let chapel_rib_b   = rotate { chapel_rib_bar } y=-0.0625
let chapel_rib_c   = rotate { chapel_rib_bar } y=-0.1250
let chapel_rib_d   = rotate { chapel_rib_bar } y=-0.1875
let chapel_rib_fan = intersection {
    translate { union { chapel_rib_bar chapel_rib_b chapel_rib_c chapel_rib_d } } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -14.10 5.10 -5.75   -7.50 5.70 -3.25
}
# The ribs are trimmed 0.12 short of the springing on the k = 0.86 line: at the springing the
# intrados turns vertical, the shell between it and its lowered copy runs to nothing, and what comes
# out is single voxels with no neighbours (one, at metre 24, measured). A rib that dies just above
# the cornice is what a rib does in any case.
let chapel_ov_086 = intersection {
    translate { scale { cylinder 0 3.90 0 r=1.0 h=7.20 axis=y } x=3.096 z=1.1610 } -10.80 0 -4.50
    box -13.95 0.25 -5.745   -7.65 7.55 -3.255
}
let chapel_ribs = intersection { chapel_rib_shell chapel_rib_fan chapel_ov_086 }

# ================================================================================================
# THE WEST APSE AND WHAT STANDS IN IT
# ================================================================================================
#
# The apse is lined in `alabaster` — the translucent stone — and the retable stands in the back
# 0.45 of it: a gesso ground with `gold_leaf` on it, a `giallo` field, `malachite` colonnettes, a
# `rosso` predella and `ormolu` mounts. Everything is held inside a cylinder of 0.70 struck on the
# apse's own axis, so no piece of it can stand outside the hollow it is meant to fill.
let chapel_ret_hold  = cylinder -14.175 3.375 -4.50  r=0.700 h=1.500 axis=y
let chapel_ret_back  = intersection { box -14.900 2.700 -5.30  -14.470 4.050 -3.70 round=0.010  chapel_ret_hold }
let chapel_ret_pred  = intersection { box -14.900 2.700 -5.30  -14.355 2.835 -3.70 round=0.010  chapel_ret_hold }
let chapel_ret_cor   = intersection { box -14.900 3.825 -5.30  -14.310 3.960 -3.70 round=0.008  chapel_ret_hold }
let chapel_ret_col_a = cylinder -14.4475 3.285 -4.905  r=0.0675 h=0.990 axis=y
let chapel_ret_col_b = cylinder -14.4475 3.285 -4.095  r=0.0675 h=0.990 axis=y
let chapel_ret_field = intersection { box -14.500 2.880 -4.815  -14.400 3.780 -4.185 round=0.008  chapel_ret_hold }
let chapel_ret_mount = intersection { box -14.470 3.735 -4.860  -14.310 3.825 -4.140 round=0.006  chapel_ret_hold }
let chapel_retable = union {
    chapel_ret_back chapel_ret_pred chapel_ret_cor
    chapel_ret_col_a chapel_ret_col_b chapel_ret_field chapel_ret_mount
}

# THE ALTAR, of `porcelain` with `silver` — translucency with a clearcoat, which the contract says
# is the combination a glazed white body actually is. It stands in the apse mouth, bedded 0.05 into
# the paving, with its mensa at 2.70 so the six tapers on it stand between 2.70 and 2.97 — below the
# dado cap, so that nothing they light reaches the cupola directly.
let chapel_alt_body  = box -14.175 1.750 -5.0625  -13.500 2.610 -3.9375 round=0.012
let chapel_alt_mensa = box -14.220 2.565 -5.1075  -13.455 2.700 -3.8925 round=0.010

# THE FRONTAL IS CARVED NOW AND WAS A FLAT SLAB. Three arches sunk 0.045 into its face — a jamb
# 0.18 wide, a semicircular head of 0.09, at 0.225 (M/2) centres — which is the smallest arcade in
# the building and the same 0.045 the vault panels next door are sunk. The sinking is a
# subtraction, so the ground of each arch is a surface that lies OUTSIDE the shape that made it:
# anything painted on it has to be keyed on a zone grown past the cut, the way rotunda.clip's note
# says and this file's apse lining already does.
let chapel_alt_arch = union {
    box      -13.4550 2.115 -0.0900   -13.4100 2.3400 0.0900
    cylinder -13.4325 2.340  0.0000  r=0.0900 h=0.045 axis=x
}
let chapel_alt_sink = union {
    translate { chapel_alt_arch } 0 0 -4.7250
    translate { chapel_alt_arch } 0 0 -4.5000
    translate { chapel_alt_arch } 0 0 -4.2750
}
let chapel_alt_front = difference {
    box -13.545 1.980 -4.9500  -13.410 2.520 -4.0500 round=0.008
    chapel_alt_sink
}
let chapel_altar = union { chapel_alt_body chapel_alt_mensa chapel_alt_front }

# ================================================================================================
# THE RAIL — `ormolu`, on x = -12.375, WITH A GATE OF 2.5 M CUT RIGHT THROUGH IT
# ================================================================================================
#
# IT WAS ON x = -13.05 AND IT HAD NO GATE, WHICH IS NOT WHAT THIS FILE SAID IT HAD. The heading
# read "with a gap of 2 M in the middle to walk through" and only the BALUSTERS had that gap: base
# and rail were two unbroken boxes from z -5.400 to -3.600, which is wall to wall in a room 1.76 m
# wide at that station. Measured, `--gap z@-13.05,1.85` on the probe reported 0.344 m of air OPEN
# at the near end — that is the air OUTSIDE the building, and none at all inside. A rail 0.45 m
# over the floor is a thing a person steps over rather than walks through, and this one ran the
# whole width of the chapel with no way past it.
#
# So the base and the top are now cut by `chapel_gate`, 1.125 (2.5 M) wide on the room's own axis,
# and the whole rail moves east from -13.05 to -12.375 — the west face of the two centre pilasters,
# which is where a rail wants to die anyway. THAT MOVE IS THE SANCTUARY. The altar's frontal stands
# at x = -13.410, so at -13.05 there was 0.315 m of floor between rail and altar and a person is
# 0.60 m wide: nobody could stand at the altar at all. At -12.375 there is 0.99 m at the frontal
# and 1.08 at the floor, which is a place to stand, and the nave loses nothing it was using — it is
# 4.725 m from here to the entrance arch, and 0.81 m clear between the two candelabra straight
# through the gate to the altar.
#
# Base and rail still run 0.05 past the dado into the wall at both ends, so each wing hangs off the
# dado and cannot come adrift however the balusters behave at a coarse metre. The balusters are
# 0.06 across at the neck — under two voxels at metre 32 and under one at metre 16, where they are
# meant to shed and the rail is meant to stay.
let chapel_gate = box -13.20 1.60 -5.0625   -12.20 2.40 -3.9375

let chapel_rail_base = difference {
    box -12.420 1.700 -5.667  -12.330 1.890 -3.333 round=0.008
    chapel_gate
}
let chapel_rail_top  = difference {
    box -12.435 2.160 -5.667  -12.315 2.250 -3.333 round=0.008
    chapel_gate
}

# THE BALUSTER IS TURNED NOW AND NOT A STICK WITH A BALL ON IT. A section in (radius, height)
# revolved about y: plinth, torus, neck, belly, collar — five members in 0.31 m, the widest 0.12
# and the neck still 0.06, which is the number the header's thin-matter list names and the one that
# has to stay. `revolve` of a drawn section is how _order.clip makes every turned thing in this
# building; a cylinder with a sphere on it was standing in for one.
let chapel_bal_sect = union {
    box    0      1.8700 -1   0.0450 1.9150 1
    bead   0.0300 1.9150     0.0525 1.9600
    box    0      1.9600 -1   0.0300 2.0400 1
    bead   0.0300 2.0400     0.0600 2.1000
    box    0      2.1000 -1   0.0375 2.1800 1
}
let chapel_bal_one = intersection {
    revolve { chapel_bal_sect } axis=y
    box -0.09 1.86 -0.09   0.09 2.19 0.09
}
# One to a wing, on the two z the old rail already used, and that is all a wing 0.368 m long in the
# clear will take: the baluster is 0.12 across the belly and the standard beside it 0.09, so a
# second one would leave 0.02 of daylight — two thirds of a voxel — and arrive as one blob.
let chapel_balusters = union {
    translate { chapel_bal_one } -12.375 0 -5.31
    translate { chapel_bal_one } -12.375 0 -3.69
}

# THE TWO GATE STANDARDS, 0.09 square and 0.09 clear of the rail's top, on the gate's own edges so
# that the 1.125 of clear width is measured between them and not through them.
let chapel_newel_one = box -12.420 1.700 -0.045   -12.330 2.340 0.045 round=0.008
let chapel_newels = union {
    translate { chapel_newel_one } 0 0 -5.1075
    translate { chapel_newel_one } 0 0 -3.8925
}
let chapel_rail = union { chapel_rail_base chapel_rail_top chapel_balusters chapel_newels }

# ================================================================================================
# TWELVE TAPERS AND TWO CANDELABRA
# ================================================================================================
#
# `wax` is translucent=150 and `taper` is emit=210. A taper is 0.045 across — one and a half voxels
# at the contract's metre — and its flame is a 0.048 x 0.09 ellipsoid overlapping the wick, so the
# two shed together at a coarse metre rather than leaving a flame in mid-air.
#
# Six stand on the altar mensa, four on brackets against the four alabaster pilasters, two on the
# candelabra. NONE OF THEM IS ABOVE 3.25. That is the point of item 2 in the header: if a flame
# stood level with the entablature it would light the cupola directly and the blind lantern would
# stop being a second-bounce problem.
# One taper, drawn with its foot on y = 0 and set down where it is wanted. It is 0.045 across and
# 0.315 tall, and the 0.045 at the bottom of it is buried in whatever it stands on — that lap is
# what stops a flame being left in mid-air when the wax sheds at a coarse metre.
# A taper is 0.045 across — 1.4 voxels at metre 32 and under one at metre 16 — so it is drawn
# standing in an `ormolu` socket 0.18 across that buries 0.09 into whatever it stands on. That
# socket is not decoration: a bare taper sheds at a coarse metre and leaves its own flame floating,
# which is what this file reported before it was there. With the socket the wax either stands on
# metal that is still there or it is gone, and nothing is ever left in mid-air.
let chapel_flame_one = ellipsoid 0 0.3450 0  rx=0.0450 ry=0.0810 rz=0.0450
let chapel_taper_one = union {
    cylinder 0 0.0225 0  r=0.0900 h=0.225 axis=y
    cylinder 0 0.2025 0  r=0.0450 h=0.315 axis=y
    chapel_flame_one
}
let chapel_tapers_altar = union {
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.950
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.815
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.680
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.320
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.185
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.050
}

# THE PILASTER BRACKETS. An `ormolu` shelf 0.135 deep on the face of each pilaster at the dado cap
# line, with a taper standing on it 0.135 clear of 0.135 of translucent stone. That distance is the
# whole of item 3: closer and the flame is inside the pilaster, further and it is a lamp in a room.
let chapel_brk_s = box -0.135 2.160 -0.045   0.135 2.250 0.135  round=0.006
let chapel_brk_n = box -0.135 2.160 -0.135   0.135 2.250 0.045  round=0.006
let chapel_brackets = union {
    translate { chapel_brk_s } -12.15 0 -5.6165
    translate { chapel_brk_s }  -9.45 0 -5.6165
    translate { chapel_brk_n } -12.15 0 -3.3835
    translate { chapel_brk_n }  -9.45 0 -3.3835
}
let chapel_tapers_brk = union {
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -12.15 2.250 -5.5490
    translate { chapel_taper_one }  -9.45 2.250 -5.5490
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -12.15 2.250 -3.4510
    translate { chapel_taper_one }  -9.45 2.250 -3.4510
}

# THE TWO STANDING CANDELABRA, flanking the altar inside the rail. Base bedded 0.09 into the paving,
# so neither can float whatever happens to the 0.09 shaft at a coarse metre.
let chapel_cand_one = union {
    cylinder 0 1.755 0  r=0.225 h=0.150 axis=y
    sphere   0 1.950 0  r=0.150
    cylinder 0 2.340 0  r=0.105 h=1.000 axis=y
    sphere   0 2.340 0  r=0.150
    cylinder 0 2.880 0  r=0.180 h=0.120 axis=y
}

let chapel_candelabra = union {
    translate { chapel_cand_one } -13.590 0 -5.13
    translate { chapel_cand_one } -13.590 0 -3.87
}
let chapel_tapers_cand = union {
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -13.590 2.940 -5.13
    translate { chapel_taper_one } -13.590 2.940 -3.87
}
let chapel_tapers = union { chapel_tapers_altar chapel_tapers_brk chapel_tapers_cand }

# THE WAX THAT HAS RUN. Twelve tapers have burned in this room and the only sign of it was that the
# wax was exactly as tall as it was cast. A pool round each socket — 0.27 across and 0.036 thick,
# half of it buried in what it stands on so it cannot be left in mid-air when the socket sheds at a
# coarse metre — and a run down the front of the mensa where the six on the altar have dripped over
# its edge. `wax` is translucent=150, so a 0.036 film over `silver` is a different question from the
# 0.09 column of it standing in the socket, and both are now in the frame.
let chapel_wax_pool = ellipsoid 0 0 0  rx=0.135 ry=0.018 rz=0.135
let chapel_wax_run  = capsule 0 2.6820 0   0 2.4300 0  r=0.0180
let chapel_wax = union {
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.950
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.815
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.680
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.320
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.185
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -14.130 2.700 -4.050
    translate { chapel_wax_run } -14.130 0 -5.1075
    translate { chapel_wax_run } -14.040 0 -5.1075
    translate { chapel_wax_run } -14.130 0 -3.8925
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -13.590 2.940 -5.130
    translate { chapel_wax_pool } -13.590 2.940 -3.870
}

# The flames on their own, so `taper` can be painted over `wax` on exactly the twelve heads and
# nowhere else. Same twelve places as above; the field is a graph, so naming the ellipsoid twice
# costs one node.
let chapel_flames = union {
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.950
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.815
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.680
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.320
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.185
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -14.130 2.700 -4.050
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -12.150 2.250 -5.5490
    translate { chapel_flame_one }  -9.450 2.250 -5.5490
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -12.150 2.250 -3.4510
    translate { chapel_flame_one }  -9.450 2.250 -3.4510
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -13.590 2.940 -5.130
    translate { chapel_flame_one } -13.590 2.940 -3.870
}

# ================================================================================================
# THE FOUR STAINED LIGHTS
# ================================================================================================
#
# 0.09 m of coloured glass, standing at z = -6.03 .. -5.94 on the south and x = -14.175 .. -14.085
# on the west. They used to stand at -5.67, which was 0.03 — one voxel — clear of a cutter that
# reached -5.70, and that clearance was the only thing keeping them in the building. It is not what
# holds them now and it never should have been: what holds each light is THIS FILE'S OWN EMBRASURE,
# whose jambs lap it by 0.0225 on each side, whose sill is 0.045 under it and whose arch is 0.0225
# over it. That is true at any cutter depth, which is why the embrasures could be cut to the wall's
# line before the cutters were fixed and why the lights could then follow the room out.
#
# WHERE THEY ARE NOW IS SET BY TWO NUMBERS AND BOTH ARE MEASURED. -5.94 is 0.09 south of the oval's
# southernmost point (-5.850), so no pane is inside the room at any x across its 1.395 of width —
# the middle one WAS, by 0.18, the moment the room moved, and came back as 1,396 floating voxels.
# -6.03 is 0.47 north of where a 1.00 cutter stops (-6.50), so no pane is inside a hole this file
# does not own.
#
# Each light is held on all four edges by stone the window cutter cannot reach: 0.045 below the
# 2.70 sill, 0.0225 above the 4.725 head, and 0.0225 into each jamb past the cutter's 1.35.
#
# The glazing bars are `lead`, 0.045 wide and standing 0.0225 proud of the glass — 1.4 voxels each
# way at metre 32 and under a voxel at metre 16, where they should go and the glass should not.
let chapel_lt_s_glass = union {
    box       -0.6975 2.655 -6.030   0.6975 4.050 -5.940 round=0.006
    cylinder   0.0000 4.050 -5.985  r=0.6975 h=0.090 axis=z
}
let chapel_lt_s_hold = union {
    box       -0.7000 2.650 -6.050   0.7000 4.050 -5.910
    cylinder   0.0000 4.050 -5.980  r=0.7000 h=0.140 axis=z
}
let chapel_lt_s_bars = intersection {
    union {
        box -0.2475 2.655 -6.030  -0.2025 4.740 -5.9175
        box  0.2025 2.655 -6.030   0.2475 4.740 -5.9175
        box -0.7000 3.015 -6.030   0.7000 3.060 -5.9175
        box -0.7000 3.375 -6.030   0.7000 3.420 -5.9175
        box -0.7000 3.735 -6.030   0.7000 3.780 -5.9175
        box -0.7000 4.050 -6.030   0.7000 4.095 -5.9175
    }
    chapel_lt_s_hold
}
let chapel_light_a = translate { chapel_lt_s_glass } -13.50 0 0
let chapel_light_b = translate { chapel_lt_s_glass } -10.80 0 0
let chapel_light_c = translate { chapel_lt_s_glass }  -8.10 0 0
let chapel_bars_s  = union {
    translate { chapel_lt_s_bars } -13.50 0 0
    translate { chapel_lt_s_bars } -10.80 0 0
    translate { chapel_lt_s_bars }  -8.10 0 0
}

# The west light, in the end wall. Its embrasure is a splay and not a tunnel, so the light sits in
# 1.20 m of wall at its north jamb and 2.19 at its south.
let chapel_light_d = union {
    box      -14.175 2.655 -6.0975  -14.085 4.050 -4.7025 round=0.006
    cylinder -14.130 4.050 -5.4000  r=0.6975 h=0.090 axis=x
}
let chapel_lt_w_hold = union {
    box      -14.190 2.650 -6.1000  -14.070 4.050 -4.7000
    cylinder -14.130 4.050 -5.4000  r=0.7000 h=0.140 axis=x
}
let chapel_bars_w = intersection {
    union {
        box -14.175 2.655 -5.6475  -14.0475 4.740 -5.6025
        box -14.175 2.655 -5.1975  -14.0475 4.740 -5.1525
        box -14.175 3.015 -6.1000  -14.0475 3.060 -4.7000
        box -14.175 3.375 -6.1000  -14.0475 3.420 -4.7000
        box -14.175 3.735 -6.1000  -14.0475 3.780 -4.7000
        box -14.175 4.050 -6.1000  -14.0475 4.095 -4.7000
    }
    chapel_lt_w_hold
}
let chapel_glass = union { chapel_light_a chapel_light_b chapel_light_c chapel_light_d }
let chapel_bars  = union { chapel_bars_s chapel_bars_w }
let chapel_glazing = union { chapel_glass chapel_bars }

# ================================================================================================
# ASSEMBLY
# ================================================================================================
#
# The mass first, then everything that stands INSIDE the air it encloses. Nothing here is bound into
# `void_chapel` except by way of the difference below, which is the whole point of that line: an
# altar, a taper or a candelabrum inside `chapel_air` and not inside the shape the manifest
# subtracts is cut away by its own room. That happened to every sconce, bench and statue in this
# building once (D608, and the long note at the foot of clips/facility.clip).
let part_chapel = union {
    chapel_mass
    chapel_dado chapel_pilasters chapel_entab chapel_fillet chapel_ribs
    chapel_stalls
    chapel_retable chapel_altar chapel_rail chapel_brackets
    chapel_candelabra chapel_tapers chapel_wax chapel_glazing
}

# THE AIR OF THIS ROOM LESS THIS ROOM'S OWN STONE, and nothing else.
let void_chapel = difference { chapel_air part_chapel }

# ================================================================================================
# THE PAVEMENT — zones, not geometry
# ================================================================================================
#
# The floor is already solid: `chapel_block` starts at 1.665 and `chapel_air` at 1.80, so the
# paving is the top 0.135 of the mass and there is nothing to build. What is needed is zones, and
# every one of them is a shape of this file's own intersected with a slab of this file's own — never
# a bare coordinate, which would reach the podium's own top surface under the whole zone.
#
# Radiating, in `rosso` on `travertine`, with a `breccia` border on the k = 0.88 line. Eight rays
# and not twenty-four: this floor is 16 m2 against the rotunda's 80, and the rotunda's own note
# about a coloured floor that becomes a carpet applies twice as hard in a room a third the size.
#
# THE GROUND UNDER THE THREE SOUTH WINDOWS IS PLAIN. That is item 1 of the header: a red patch
# thrown by a ruby light has to land on something that is not already red, or there is nothing to
# measure. The rays stop at the k = 0.88 line and the border is the only colour that reaches the
# wall, so the 1.0 m of embrasure floor under each light is bare travertine.
let chapel_fl_slab   = box -15.14 1.660 -6.64   -6.01 1.815 -2.96
let chapel_fl_all    = intersection { chapel_mass chapel_fl_slab }
let chapel_fl_border = intersection { difference { chapel_ov_100 chapel_ov_088 } chapel_fl_slab }
let chapel_fl_field  = intersection { chapel_ov_088 chapel_fl_slab }
let chapel_fl_disc   = intersection { chapel_ov_030 chapel_fl_slab }

let chapel_ray_bar = mirror { box 0.300 1.655 -0.045   3.700 1.830 0.045 } axis=x
let chapel_ray_b   = rotate { chapel_ray_bar } y=-0.0625
let chapel_ray_c   = rotate { chapel_ray_bar } y=-0.1250
let chapel_ray_d   = rotate { chapel_ray_bar } y=-0.1875
let chapel_fl_rays = intersection {
    translate { union { chapel_ray_bar chapel_ray_b chapel_ray_c chapel_ray_d } } -10.80 0 -4.50
    chapel_fl_field
}

# The apse lining, keyed on a band that CONTAINS the stone behind the hollow rather than on the
# hollow itself. rotunda.clip's note: a surface made by a subtraction lies OUTSIDE the thing that
# subtracted it, so a rule keyed on a hole misses half the lining at any metre coarser than the
# contract's. This zone is the apse cutter grown by 0.18 and clipped short of the paving.
let chapel_apse_zone = intersection {
    union {
        cylinder  -14.175 2.700 -4.50  r=0.855 h=1.800 axis=y
        ellipsoid -14.175 3.600 -4.50  rx=0.855 ry=0.630 rz=0.855
    }
    box -15.16 1.860 -5.42   -13.40 4.32 -3.58
}

# ================================================================================================
# PAINT
# ================================================================================================
#
# Every rule names a shape bound in this file and every one tests below=0.02 and not below=0,
# because the manifest displaces the whole building by 12 mm of grain at the end and a voxel that
# exists only because the grain pushed the surface out lies OUTSIDE the shape that made it.
#
# The order is: the room white, then the floor, then the order, then the hollows, then what stands
# in them, then the glass — a later coat covers an earlier one.

paint plaster where=chapel_mass below=0.02

paint travertine where=chapel_fl_all    below=0.02
paint breccia    where=chapel_fl_border below=0.02
paint rosso      where=chapel_fl_rays   below=0.02
paint rosso      where=chapel_fl_disc   below=0.02

paint travertine where=chapel_dado      below=0.02
paint gilt       where=chapel_dado_cap  below=0.02

# THE FOUR PILASTERS ARE THE TRANSLUCENT STONE AND THE CAPITALS ARE NOT. `alabaster` at
# translucent=210 against `gilt` at metal=225 on the same member, 0.135 apart, with a flame at its
# foot: whatever the subsurface term does it has to do it beside a metal that does not do it.
paint alabaster  where=chapel_pilasters below=0.02
paint gilt       where=chapel_capitals  below=0.02

paint plaster    where=chapel_entab     below=0.02
paint gilt       where=chapel_fillet    below=0.02
paint gilt       where=chapel_ribs      below=0.02
paint alabaster  where=chapel_apse_zone below=0.02

# THE STALLS: `walnut` for the joinery, `gold_leaf` on the ground of the sunk panels, and `ebony`
# where hands and backs have been. The panel rule is keyed on the sink GROWN BY 0.03 and not on the
# sink itself, for the reason the apse lining gives above — the ground of a sunk panel is a surface
# made by a subtraction and lies outside the shape that cut it.
#
# The wear is not decoration either. A stall seat and the arm beside it are the two places in a
# chapel a hand and a body are always on the wood, and worn walnut is darker and smoother than
# walnut, which is what `ebony` at rough=120 is beside `walnut` at 180. The zone is the top 0.045 of
# the seat and the top 0.09 of the arms — where a person is, and nowhere else.
paint walnut     where=chapel_stalls    below=0.02
let chapel_stall_pan_zone = round { chapel_stall_sink } by=0.030
paint gold_leaf  where=chapel_stall_pan_zone below=0.02
let chapel_stall_wear = union {
    intersection { chapel_stall_seat box -11.925 2.250 -5.905  -9.675 2.295 -3.095 }
    intersection { chapel_stall_arms box -11.925 2.385 -5.905  -9.675 2.475 -3.095 }
}
paint ebony      where=chapel_stall_wear below=0.02

# THE RETABLE: five materials on a surface 1.35 wide. gesso is the ground gold leaf is laid on and
# it is under it here for the same reason it is in a workshop — `gold_leaf` at rough=40 is the
# brightest of the contract's three golds, `ormolu` at 48 the next and `gilt` at 64 the dullest, and
# all three are inside two metres of each other in this apse so the difference between them is a
# comparison the eye can make in one frame rather than a claim about one sample.
paint gesso      where=chapel_ret_back  below=0.02
paint rosso      where=chapel_ret_pred  below=0.02
paint ormolu     where=chapel_ret_cor   below=0.02
paint malachite  where=chapel_ret_col_a below=0.02
paint malachite  where=chapel_ret_col_b below=0.02
paint giallo     where=chapel_ret_field below=0.02
paint gold_leaf  where=chapel_ret_mount below=0.02

paint porcelain  where=chapel_altar        below=0.02
paint silver     where=chapel_alt_mensa    below=0.02
paint silver     where=chapel_alt_front    below=0.02
# The three sunk arches read as white bays in a silver frontal, and the rule is keyed on the sink
# grown by 0.03 because the ground of a sunk panel lies outside the shape that cut it.
let chapel_alt_arch_zone = round { chapel_alt_sink } by=0.030
paint porcelain  where=chapel_alt_arch_zone below=0.02
paint ormolu     where=chapel_rail         below=0.02
paint ormolu     where=chapel_brackets     below=0.02
paint ormolu     where=chapel_candelabra   below=0.02

# THE TWELVE TAPERS. `wax` is translucent=150 and stands 0.09 into an `ormolu` socket, so the socket
# is painted first and the wax over it; the flame is the last coat of all, and it is `taper` at
# emit=210 — the hottest emissive in the contract, in a shape 0.09 across.
paint wax        where=chapel_tapers    below=0.02
paint wax        where=chapel_wax       below=0.02
paint taper      where=chapel_flames    below=0.02

# THE FOUR STAINED LIGHTS. Three colours, and which one is where is the whole of the measurement:
# `glass_ruby` in the west window over the altar and in the west-most south light, so the two
# strongest reds land on the sanctuary floor and the apse; `glass_gold` in the middle, over the
# body of the room; `glass_blue` in the east light, next to the door. Ruby and blue are four metres
# apart with the white cupola over both of them.
paint glass_ruby where=chapel_light_a   below=0.02
paint glass_gold where=chapel_light_b   below=0.02
paint glass_blue where=chapel_light_c   below=0.02
paint glass_ruby where=chapel_light_d   below=0.02
paint lead       where=chapel_bars      below=0.02

# NO WEATHERING. This is an interior, it has never been rained on, and the manifest already spends
# 12 mm of grain on the whole building. A second displacement here would eat the 0.0225 step between
# architrave and frieze and the 0.045 glazing bars, which are half of what this room is for.
